    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d141">

                <pb facs="0251" n="156" sameAs="frus:frus1936v04/pg156"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/7908: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Peck</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-05-18T12:00:00+08:00">May 18, 1936—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received
                        9:10 p.m.<anchor n="83" xml:id="d141-f1"/>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>143. 1. An official of the Foreign Office this morning read to an officer of the
                    Embassy portion of a telegram stated to have just been received from Chinese
                    Ambassador at Washington to effect that in connection with Japanese activities
                    in North China, presumably including smuggling and increase of Tientsin Japanese
                    Garrison, Under Secretary had issued statement to press reaffirming American
                    position in respect to North China developments as set forth in the Secretary’s
                    statement of December 5, 1935.<anchor n="84" xml:id="d141-f2"/></p>
                <p>2. To Department and Peiping.</p>
                <p>3. Same official stated that Foreign Office on May 15 or 16 handed to Japanese
                    Embassy a lengthy note concerning smuggling in North <pb facs="0252" n="157"
                        xml:id="pg157"/> China which is being published in the press and which
                    recapitulates the smuggling activities and previous representations and asks
                    that the Japanese Government take immediate steps to restrain the Japanese
                    military authorities from further interference with the principal functions of
                    the customs and to withdraw the protection “hitherto enjoyed by Japanese and
                    Korean smugglers”.</p>
                <p>4. We have a translation of this note and if it is not published in the United
                    States and the Department desires to receive the text by radio, instructions to
                    that effect will be appreciated.</p>
                <p>5. To Peiping by mail.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Peck</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
